WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.746
Five of the elements never change.

00:00:02.746 --> 00:00:07.753
But that's only exactly true if the Earth is a perfect sphere.

00:00:07.753 --> 00:00:08.480
It isn't.

00:00:09.628 --> 00:00:12.464
Earth spins, so it bulges at the equator.

00:00:12.464 --> 00:00:16.268
It's about twenty one kilometers wider than it is tall.

00:00:16.601 --> 00:00:20.820
Your notes add one correction term to the gravity potential.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:27.641
Its size is set by a single number, J two, about one point oh eight times ten to the minus three.

00:00:28.575 --> 00:00:34.836
The extra mass at the equator tugs a tilted orbit, trying to pull its plane down toward the equator.

00:00:34.836 --> 00:00:37.215
Like a hand pushing on a spinning top.

00:00:37.541 --> 00:00:41.173
And like a top, the orbit doesn't fall flat.

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:46.621
It precesses: the whole orbit plane slowly swings around the pole.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:01:00.000
The drift rate of the node is Omega dot equals minus three n J two R squared, over two a squared, times one minus e squared, squared, times cosine i.

00:01:00.284 --> 00:01:01.583
Read the cosine i.

00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:08.437
For a prograde orbit, cosine i is positive, so Omega dot is negative: the node slides westward.

00:01:08.437 --> 00:01:12.044
For the space station, about five degrees per day.

00:01:12.328 --> 00:01:17.809
For a polar orbit, cosine i is zero, and the node doesn't drift at all.

00:01:17.809 --> 00:01:21.128
For a retrograde orbit, it drifts eastward.

00:01:21.395 --> 00:01:24.377
Engineers use that eastward drift on purpose.

00:01:24.377 --> 00:01:32.595
If the node turns exactly once per year, about one degree per day, the orbit plane keeps the same angle to the Sun all year.

00:01:32.881 --> 00:01:35.035
That's a sun synchronous orbit.

00:01:35.035 --> 00:01:40.801
At seven hundred kilometers, it needs an inclination of about ninety eight degrees.

00:01:41.072 --> 00:01:43.881
Meanwhile a, e and i don't drift.

00:01:43.881 --> 00:01:49.072
They only wiggle a little each orbit, like your notes sketch.

00:01:49.372 --> 00:01:50.059
Your turn.

00:01:50.059 --> 00:01:53.972
Pause here and do checkpoint six point one in your guide.

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:02.527
J two turns the ellipse inside its plane too.

00:02:02.527 --> 00:02:17.479
The perigee drifts at omega dot equals minus three n J two R squared over two a squared, one minus e squared, squared, times five halves sine squared i, minus two.

00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:20.780
The sign depends on that last bracket.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.939
Here it is, plotted against inclination.

00:02:24.272 --> 00:02:25.054
Your turn.

00:02:25.054 --> 00:02:31.312
Pause here and do checkpoint six point two: find where the bracket crosses zero.

00:02:34.939 --> 00:02:38.929
It crosses zero when five halves sine squared i equals two.

00:02:38.929 --> 00:02:44.339
So sine squared i is four fifths, and i is sixty three point four three degrees.

00:02:45.339 --> 00:02:49.374
At that inclination, the perigee simply stops turning.

00:02:49.374 --> 00:02:52.139
It's called the critical inclination.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.963
Now look back at the hook.

00:02:54.963 --> 00:02:59.690
The Molniya orbit was inclined at sixty three point four degrees.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.072
That's no accident.

00:03:02.006 --> 00:03:12.659
At that inclination, the perigee stays parked over the south, so the long, slow apogee stays over Russia, year after year, with no fuel spent.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:20.686
Its node still drifts a little, about point one five degrees per day, but that doesn't matter for coverage.

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:22.723
Let's recap.

00:03:22.723 --> 00:03:30.959
The E C I frame points I at the vernal equinox and K at the North Pole, and it doesn't spin with the Earth.

00:03:31.259 --> 00:03:45.539
Six elements place any orbit: a and e for size and shape, three turns for orientation, capital Omega about K, i about the node line, little omega about h, and theta for where the satellite is.

00:03:46.553 --> 00:03:53.757
Those three turns are the rows of C P N, C three of omega, C one of i, C three of Omega.

00:03:53.757 --> 00:03:58.833
The angles come back out with an arc cosine and two atan twos.

00:03:59.166 --> 00:04:07.081
From r and v: energy gives a, h and the eccentricity vector give e and the frame, and r dot v settles theta.

00:04:07.081 --> 00:04:09.206
The transpose takes you back.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:18.946
And Earth's bulge slowly turns the node and the perigee, except at the critical inclination, where the perigee stands still.
